            {
                "entry_id": "524f7729-6a7d-331f-9cf8-2702bd41a15d",
                "word": "théomorphe",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "fr",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "fe273503-7276-4b0e-a899-22170b465c43",
                        "entry_id": "524f7729-6a7d-331f-9cf8-2702bd41a15d",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural",
                            "masculine",
                            "feminine"
                        ],
                        "form": "théomorphes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "10749789-85e5-3e86-9af1-1764d2eb70e4",
                        "entry_id": "524f7729-6a7d-331f-9cf8-2702bd41a15d",
                        "glosses": [
                            "À l’image de Dieu."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "ba0fde03-b14e-4353-9fde-6170a6b5b297",
                                "sense_id": "10749789-85e5-3e86-9af1-1764d2eb70e4",
                                "text": "Selon l’ordre de la Création, la masculinité est « théomorphe » (à l’image de Dieu), tandis que la femme, « en tant que telle », ne peut l’être."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e22c323f-6166-40ab-b93a-3c9365a3afe8",
                                "sense_id": "10749789-85e5-3e86-9af1-1764d2eb70e4",
                                "text": "À côté des portraits en pied du Princeps et des membres de sa famille exposés dans les espaces publics des cités de l’Empire, qui déclinaient un petit nombre de types iconographiques stéréotypés relevant des sphères civile, religieuse ou militaire (togati, statues cuirassées, statues équestres notamment) et proposaient une vision actualisée des dispositifs familiaux et dynastiques, une catégorie spécifique est constituée par les images que l’on peut qualifier de manière générique d’héroïsantes ou divinisantes, qui associent une tête-portrait à un corps idéal (on les appellera aujourd’hui plus volontiers « théomorphes » car le terme ne préjuge pas du sens qu’il convient de leur donner) ; elles ont récemment davantage retenu l’attention des chercheurs, de M. Bergmann à A. Alexandridis."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "7306ca2a-5a4d-4213-a7d7-17c7f2b4aadc",
                        "source_entry_id": "524f7729-6a7d-331f-9cf8-2702bd41a15d",
                        "source_sense_id": "10749789-85e5-3e86-9af1-1764d2eb70e4",
                        "target_lang_word": "theomorphic",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "theomorphic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "68ea106b-cf70-3071-93d4-8e549ef6546f",
                                        "entry_id": "4dd04629-9abe-319b-9334-685e994c74f5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Having the form of God or a god."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "06a335d5-cb7c-43f7-bf0c-a110a8cb71b2",
                                                "sense_id": "68ea106b-cf70-3071-93d4-8e549ef6546f",
                                                "text": "Man is a theomorphic being; endowed with the intelligence (al-'aql) which can lead him to the truth, to knowledge of Allah and to unity (tawhid)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "cf8d1bee-290e-4eed-9f40-6991f4c666ef",
                                                "sense_id": "68ea106b-cf70-3071-93d4-8e549ef6546f",
                                                "text": "That Jesus is male furthers the difficulties already encountered in relation to god the Father, seemingly identifying men as not only more theomorphic (that is, like God) than women, but also as significantly more Christomorphic (that is, like Christ)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3f747278-e542-481f-a39b-7bdb2ec54da3",
                                                "sense_id": "68ea106b-cf70-3071-93d4-8e549ef6546f",
                                                "text": "Correspondingly, the first male, Adam, is defined as Godlike human prototype, whereas the first female, Eve, is derived and therefore not theomorphic."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "74f125d6-cb59-344a-9609-71896e61a76a",
                                        "entry_id": "4dd04629-9abe-319b-9334-685e994c74f5",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of, pertaining to or involving the conception of man as having the form of God or a god."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c681a2a4-8966-4c64-b1d4-bd219f382ea9",
                                                "sense_id": "74f125d6-cb59-344a-9609-71896e61a76a",
                                                "text": "In this paper I will offer some reflections on interpretations of theomorphic themes in Islamic thought."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "7fac81e1-60f4-4cdf-8d6b-66ef41e9f71b",
                                                "sense_id": "74f125d6-cb59-344a-9609-71896e61a76a",
                                                "text": "It seems that the more remote from scientific realization is the idea that the philosopher is searching to grasp, the more theomorphic the explanation will tend to be."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e4b53faa-3bbf-44aa-9f61-5eb8ba9c3bcc",
                                                "sense_id": "74f125d6-cb59-344a-9609-71896e61a76a",
                                                "text": "2015, Mikhail Epstein, Post-Atheism: From Apophatic Theology to \"Minimal Religion\", Mikhail N. Epstein, Alexander A. Genis, Slobodanka Vladiv-Glover, Russian Postmodernism: New Perspectives on Post-Soviet Culture, page 453,\nIn the theomorphic representation there is no trace of God, but the locus and form of his absence are precisely indicated as the “highest or most powerful value,” even if this value is a negative one."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
